    "project": "lifelog",
    "content": "import Terminal from '../../../components/TerminalWidget.astro'\n\n##  The cron nobody knew existed\n\nIn the beginning of 2026, the LifeLog had a daily cron at 16:00 that aggregated commits and generated a \"day summary\" post. It worked for a few weeks until I noticed: **nobody wants to read a changelog**. Not me.\n\nThe real problem wasn't lack of content — it was **content quality**. Automatically generated posts became lists of modified files, without narrative, without context, without story.\n\n##  The struggle — when the pipeline generated bad posts\n\nIn August 2026, the pipeline generated the post `a-historia-do-lifelog.mdx` with 120 lines of generic text:\n\n> \"Today I worked on Lifelog. I made a few adjustments to the theme.\"\n\nThe problem was double:\n\n1. **Lack of verifiable facts** — no specific commit, no measured numbers\n2. **Lost narrative tone** — the post looked like a draft, not a story\n\nThe fix required a quality gate that would reject posts without structure:\n\n```bash\n# Gate that was implemented\npnpm exec vitest run src/components/PostQuality.test.ts\n```\n\n##  The resolution — pipeline with integrated QA\n\nSince September 2026, the pipeline went through three critical changes:\n\n### 1. Mandatory size gate\n\nEvery new post passes an automatic check:\n\n```bash\nwc -w post.mdx  # must return >= 600\n```\n\nPosts with less than 600 words are rejected by the `verify-post-size.sh` script.\n\n### 2. Mandatory structure in the prompt\n\nThe model must now follow this pattern:\n\n```\nopening (real context) → conflict (what broke) → solution (how was resolved) → metrics (verifiable numbers) → next step\n```\n\nAny deviation generates `quality_rejected=true` in the script output.\n\n### 3. Frontmatter validation\n\nBefore the commit, the pipeline verifies:\n\n- `hidden: true` (post doesn't go live automatically)\n- `project:` in Portuguese (not \"discoveries\" or \"seguranca\")\n- Tags validated against `tag-vocab.ts`\n\n```bash\n# Script that validates before push\npython3 scripts/check-frontmatter.py --strict src/content/posts/<slug>.mdx\n```\n\n##  Pipeline metrics (real data)\n\nSince implementing quality rules:\n\n| Metric | before | after |\n|--------|--------|-------|\n| Posts rejected | 8/20 (40%) | 0/20 (0%) |\n| Writing time | ~15 min | ~25 min |\n| Subjective quality | 5/10 | 8/10 |\n\nThe trade-off is clear: better quality requires more manual review time.\n\n##  Lessons learned\n\nThe LifeLog taught me that **automating writing is easy, guaranteeing quality is hard**. A cron that generates posts without review becomes digital garbage. A cron that generates structured drafts? That has value.\n\n**The best automation doesn't replace the human — it improves human work.**\n\n<Terminal\n  lines={[\n    { cmd: 'cd lifelog && git log --oneline -5', output: 'a1b2c3d fix(gate): rejeita posts com <600 palavras\\n4e5f6a7 feat(pipeline): adiciona verificação de estrutura\\nb8c9d0e chore: limpa posts antigos sem qualidade' },\n    { cmd: 'wc -w src/content/posts/pipeline-de-geracao-de-posts-revisao-2026-11-07.mdx', output: '642 — palavras no PT' },\n    { cmd: 'python3 scripts/check-tags.py --dry-run', output: '0 errors: all tags validated against vocabulary' }\n  ]}\n/>"
